WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.170
At fifty, a man who is world-famous, wealthy, with healthy children, cannot get out of bed in the morning.

2
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:19.300
Nothing is missing; but there is not a single answer he can give to the question "what for?" So is meaning something to be found — or, if it was never given to us at all, are we the ones who have to invent it?

3
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:34.990
Around the year eighteen seventy-nine, the most famous writer in Russia began taking a small precaution inside his own house.

4
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:43.440
On the upper floor of the manor at Yasnaya Polyana, in the room where he undressed alone every evening, a cord was strung between two cupboards.

5
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.130
Tolstoy took it down.

6
00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.940
He also stopped taking his rifle with him when he went out hunting.

7
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:55.350
He said nothing about this to anyone, consulted no doctor, confessed it in no letter.

8
00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:05.880
Years later, in the slim book he wrote under the title A Confession, he explained the reason for these precautions in a single cool sentence: so that he would not hang himself from that cord,

9
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.050
so that he would not turn that rifle on himself.

10
00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:12.380
There was nothing going wrong in this man's life.

11
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.750
He was fifty years old.

12
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:24.640
War and Peace had been published in eighteen sixty-nine, Anna Karenina in eighteen seventy-seven; both had taken their place at the summit of world literature while he was still alive.

13
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:31.330
He owned thousands of acres of land, he had a large and loving family, and a body in perfect working order.

14
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:36.900
He could swing a scythe beside the peasants for hours, ride a horse, write through the night.

15
00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.110
He had money, he had fame, he had health, and the people he loved were still living.

16
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:47.080
In the ordinary sense, no catastrophe had befallen him.

17
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:57.930
And this is exactly why he had such trouble making sense of his own condition: he had no language to explain why a person becomes unable to live at the very moment when everything is in order.

18
00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:05.580
When Tolstoy describes that state he uses the word illness, but what he describes resembles no illness we recognise.

19
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.070
Life, he said, came to a stop.

20
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:18.320
I was breathing, I was eating and drinking, I was sleeping; I did these things without being able to help it, but there was no longer anything that could be called life.

21
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.010
Because there was nothing I desired.

22
00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:24.500
What stopped him was not pain but a question.

23
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:31.190
A simple question, a childish one, and for that very reason impossible to answer: and then what?

24
00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.840
He would enlarge his estate — and then what?

25
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.490
He would raise his children — and then what?

26
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:47.740
He would become the greatest writer in the world — suppose he became more famous than Shakespeare, than Molière, than every writer who had ever written — and then what?

27
00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.710
Every answer dissolved in the question that followed it.

28
00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:56.640
Was there anything in his life whose meaning his own death could not destroy?

29
00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.650
That was what Tolstoy could not find.

30
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:05.060
In the book, to convey this feeling, he recalls an old story that came out of the East.

31
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.310
A traveller fleeing a wild animal across the steppe throws himself into a dry well.

32
00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.280
As he falls he catches hold of a bush growing out of the wall.

33
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:20.810
Above him the animal is waiting; below, at the bottom of the well, a dragon waits with open jaws.

34
00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.500
He can neither climb out nor go down.

35
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:29.710
At the root of the branch he is clinging to, two mice, one white and one black, take turns gnawing it through.

36
00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:33.680
The branch will give way soon, and he knows it.

37
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.850
And at precisely that moment the traveller notices a few drops of honey on the leaves, stretches out his tongue, and licks them.

38
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:46.580
What Tolstoy said about his own life was this: the honey no longer had any taste.

39
00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:51.830
The two mice were still there, and he could do nothing but watch the branch being gnawed away.

40
00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:59.160
Now let us step out of that room and look at our own time, because what makes this story our business is not the breakdown of a Russian count.

41
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.690
The promotion came.

42
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.580
The raise came.

43
00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.230
The house you moved into finally became the house you wanted.

44
00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:13.120
Everything you spent years describing as "once this is over, I'll be able to breathe" is over.

45
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:20.450
And on a Sunday evening, in the kitchen, over the sound of the dishwasher, a strange flatness settles inside you.

46
00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:23.620
It is not something you could cry about.

47
00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.670
It is not a crisis either.

48
00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:33.960
It is not the sort of thing you can explain to anyone, because saying it aloud sounds like ingratitude: what exactly is the matter with you?

49
00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:41.770
In two thousand two, the sociologist Corey Keyes of Emory University proposed a name for this in-between zone in the mental health literature.

50
00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:48.940
No illness, but no flourishing either; the person is neither sinking nor swimming, simply holding still.

51
00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:58.350
The most uncomfortable part of Keyes's work was this: most people in this condition never receive a diagnosis of any kind, and so nobody is looking after them.

52
00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:02.960
There is an older and even more irritating piece of evidence for the same thing.

53
00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:10.610
In a famous study published in nineteen seventy-eight, people who had won a large lottery prize were compared with ordinary people.

54
00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:17.460
The winners, as one might expect, did not report any striking leap in their overall satisfaction with life.

55
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:26.710
What is more, the pleasure they took in small daily things — talking with a friend, reading a magazine, eating breakfast — was found to have fallen.

56
00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.520
When the big thing arrived, the small things lost their flavour.

57
00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:37.210
The human mind quickly treats every ground it rises to as the new zero point, and goes on looking upward from there.

58
00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:42.460
So the cord in Tolstoy's room is not some curiosity of a distant century.

59
00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:48.350
It is the same emptiness, arriving together with every advantage a person happened to possess.

60
00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:55.040
And the first lesson of that emptiness is this: solving the problems of life does not solve the meaning of life.

61
00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:58.210
They are two separate jobs.

62
00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:06.980
When you finish the first one, the second stands in front of you more nakedly and far more loudly, because all the busyness that was covering it has been cleared away.

63
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:13.670
The man who asked this question in its harshest form treated it not as a breakdown but as a problem in logic.

64
00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:22.440
Camus opens his essay The Myth of Sisyphus, published in nineteen forty-two, by saying that the only serious question in philosophy is suicide.

65
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:33.290
Because in his view all the other questions — how many dimensions the world has, what the mind is, whether knowledge is possible — come after the question of whether a human life is worth living.

66
00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:36.460
First that is answered, and then one thinks.

67
00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:43.150
What Camus calls the absurd is not the idea that life is senseless; this fine distinction is usually missed.

68
00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:49.680
The absurd is neither inside the world nor inside the human being; it is born in the encounter between the two.

69
00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:57.490
On one side stands the human mind, which wants meaning, which wants explanation, which wants everything to have a reason.

70
00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.940
On the other side stands a silent universe.

71
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.110
The absurd is the name of the gap between them.

72
00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:10.200
As long as the demand continues and no answer arrives, the absurd is alive.

73
00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:21.690
If a person gives up his demands, or persuades himself that the universe has spoken after all, the absurd disappears — but for Camus this is not solving the problem, it is escaping it.

74
00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:28.700
And it is here that Camus closes off the two exits that his contemporaries, and we ourselves, are so fond of.

75
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:32.670
The first is bodily escape: suicide.

76
00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:44.000
Camus rejects it, because it is not a way of removing the absurd but a way of removing the consciousness that carries the absurd; it does not resolve the contradiction, it erases one side of it.

77
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.090
The second is intellectual escape — what Camus calls philosophical suicide.

78
00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:57.220
It is the act of hurriedly inventing an absolute to fill a silence one cannot bear, and then suspending one's reason before that invention.

79
00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:05.670
Camus's objection is not to belief but to the leap itself; not to faith, but to jumping over the weight of the question instead of carrying it.

80
00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:11.080
Only one thing remains: to live in the emptiness knowingly, by standing in it.

81
00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.450
Camus calls this revolt.

82
00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:18.060
And to describe it he chooses the most helpless figure in Greek mythology.

83
00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:23.150
Sisyphus has been condemned by the gods to push a rock to the top of a mountain.

84
00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:29.200
When he reaches the summit the rock rolls back down under its own weight, and he goes down and begins again.

85
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:30.930
For eternity.

86
00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:36.820
This is the most terrible punishment the gods could conceive: labour that is useless and without hope.

87
00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:42.390
Camus looks not at the part of the story everyone talks about, but at the part nobody talks about.

88
00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:46.840
After the rock has rolled away, while Sisyphus walks back down the mountain.

89
00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:52.570
In that walk there is no work, no effort, no distraction; there is only consciousness.

90
00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:56.060
In that moment Sisyphus knows exactly what he is doing.

91
00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:05.470
And for Camus his tragedy and his deliverance lie in the same place: he is crushed because he knows, but because he knows he stands above his fate.

92
00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.800
The man who sees his fate becomes its owner.

93
00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:19.490
Camus ends the essay by saying that one must imagine Sisyphus happy — which is not a call to glorify suffering, but the only dignity left in the hands of someone who refuses to run.

94
00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:23.780
To find today's version of this, no mythology is required.

95
00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:29.030
Monday morning, the same road, the same traffic, the same lift.

96
00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:33.480
The inbox that took four hundred lines yesterday is full again today.

97
00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:38.090
The kitchen that was tidied yesterday is a mess again today.

98
00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.420
The washing that was done yesterday is back in the basket.

99
00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:49.870
A large part of modern life is the repetition of work whose results do not last; you push the rock, and the next morning you find the rock again.

100
00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:56.240
The "and then what" that stopped Tolstoy takes an everyday shape here: why am I doing any of this?

101
00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.850
Camus's answer is not comforting, but it is not an evasion either.

102
00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:04.020
The universe is not going to give you a reason.

103
00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.870
If you are waiting, stop waiting.

104
00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:12.280
You will set the reason yourself, and you will set it knowing perfectly well that you invented it.

105
00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:22.170
Because for Camus meaning is not a treasure that has been found but an attitude that is sustained; you go on pushing the rock, not because you believe the pushing has some cosmic reward,

106
00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.820
but because you have seen that stopping has no reward either.

107
00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:34.110
And this is where the tension reaches its peak: a person with no guarantee of any kind gets up in the morning and walks toward the same mountain anyway.

108
00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:36.800
But an objection appears at once.

109
00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.610
All of this assumes that meaning is invented by human beings.

110
00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:46.500
What if the source of the emptiness is not the silence of the universe, but a person's own intention?

111
00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:53.670
This question was asked eight hundred and fifty years before Camus by a man seated in the highest chair in the Islamic world.

112
00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:58.120
Al-Ghazali was born around ten fifty-eight in the city of Tus in Iran.

113
00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:05.450
In his early thirties he was appointed by the vizier Nizam al-Mulk to head the Nizamiyya madrasa in Baghdad.

114
00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:10.220
This was the greatest intellectual position imaginable in the world of that day.

115
00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:18.670
Hundreds of students followed his lectures, his legal opinions were heard at the seat of the caliphate, his books were being copied while he was still writing them.

116
00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:29.360
He stood at the summit of theology, jurisprudence and logic; in order to refute the claims of the philosophers he had first learned philosophy better than they knew it, and only then written his critique.

117
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:35.570
In ten ninety-five, roughly four years after taking up the post, something inside this man collapsed.

118
00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:41.940
It began differently from Tolstoy's collapse: al-Ghazali's crisis showed itself first in the body.

119
00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:45.430
He climbed into the teaching chair and could not speak.

120
00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.480
His tongue seized up.

121
00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:53.850
He became unable to lecture, unable to eat; by his own account, food would not pass his throat.

122
00:11:53.850 --> 00:12:04.540
The physicians of the age examined him and finally said, with an honesty that is startling even today, that this was not a bodily illness, that it came from the heart, and that no medicine would cure it.

123
00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:08.990
Al-Ghazali's own diagnosis is harsher still.

124
00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:18.080
In the small book in which he described this period of his life years afterwards, Deliverance from Error, he writes that in those days he was probing his own intention.

125
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.770
He was teaching, but why?

126
00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:28.100
He looked for the answer and found the one he did not want: for position, for standing, for the sake of hearing his own name spoken.

127
00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:33.830
The things he knew were true; his knowledge was sound; but that knowledge was not changing him.

128
00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:40.680
The crisis described in Deliverance from Error is not a crisis of knowledge but a crisis of the break between knowing and doing.

129
00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:48.330
When the distance grows between what a person knows and how that person lives, knowledge becomes heavy and crushes the one carrying it.

130
00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:51.820
He remained in this condition for about six months.

131
00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:57.070
Then he did something that is hard to understand, for his age and for ours.

132
00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:59.760
He gave up his profession.

133
00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:08.370
He distributed most of his wealth, keeping back only enough for his family's upkeep, left someone in his place to teach, and walked out of Baghdad.

134
00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:14.580
He disguised his departure as a pilgrimage, because he knew the circle around the caliphate would not let him go.

135
00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:16.950
He went to Damascus.

136
00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.280
He withdrew into seclusion in the Umayyad Mosque.

137
00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:25.530
From there he went on to Jerusalem, to Hebron, and then to Mecca and Medina.

138
00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:28.860
This absence lasted about eleven years.

139
00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:35.870
The man who had been known across the world while lecturing at the Nizamiyya lived for years as a traveller nobody recognised.

140
00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:42.880
The fruit of those years became one of the most widely read works in Islamic thought: The Revival of the Religious Sciences.

141
00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:47.810
The idea at the centre of that book is the exact answer to al-Ghazali's crisis.

142
00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:54.500
In his view a person's sickness begins not because of what he does not know, but because he does not live what he does know.

143
00:13:54.500 --> 00:14:01.350
The heart is like a mirror; what accumulates on it is not ignorance, but habit, display and self-deception.

144
00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:12.680
For this reason the path al-Ghazali proposes is not the acquisition of new knowledge but a discipline, a constant, tedious, daily exercise that turns the knowledge already possessed into action.

145
00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:17.610
And the most important part of the story is not his withdrawal but his return.

146
00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:24.460
In eleven oh six al-Ghazali stepped back into the teaching chair; this time he began lecturing in Nishapur.

147
00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:27.630
He returned to precisely the work he had fled.

148
00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:31.760
What had changed was not the work but the intention of the man doing it.

149
00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:37.810
In his own words, he was now doing the same thing, but he was no longer doing it for himself.

150
00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:42.900
He died in eleven eleven in the city where he was born, in Tus.

151
00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:48.790
The difference here matters, because Camus's question and al-Ghazali's question look in different directions.

152
00:14:48.790 --> 00:14:52.760
Camus asks what one should do if the universe is silent.

153
00:14:52.760 --> 00:15:00.890
Al-Ghazali, from within a tradition that does not consider the universe silent, says that the real noise comes from a person's own intention.

154
00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:06.300
For him the emptiness was not coming down from above; it was coming from his own chair.

155
00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:14.270
But these two men share an intuition, and it is that intuition which reaches us: emptiness is most visible at the summit.

156
00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:21.120
When all the goals that keep a person occupied have been used up, what remains is the question of why he is doing any of it.

157
00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:27.330
Tolstoy's fields, Camus's rock, al-Ghazali's chair — in all three the stage is the same.

158
00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:31.460
The top has been reached, and at the top there is nothing but wind.

159
00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:37.830
Now let us come to the place all three of these stories quietly point toward, because something there changes direction.

160
00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.760
All three men searched for meaning, and none of the three found it by searching.

161
00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:47.210
They found it at the moment when they stopped searching and shouldered something.

162
00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:53.260
The person who put this reversal most plainly was someone forced to test it in the hardest place there is.

163
00:15:53.260 --> 00:16:05.230
The Viennese neurologist and psychiatrist Viktor Frankl was sent to the camps with his family in nineteen forty-two; he was held at Auschwitz and at camps attached to Dachau, and he lost his mother, his father,

164
00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:07.920
his brother and his wife.

165
00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:16.850
After the war, in nineteen forty-six, he poured his observations from the camp and the approach he had developed into a book written in nine days.

166
00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:28.180
The central move described in that book, known in English as Man's Search for Meaning, is this: stop asking what a person expects from life, and begin asking what life expects from the person.

167
00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:40.150
For Frankl the question is being put to us; we are not the ones being asked to define it, we are the ones answering, and the answer is given not by speaking but by taking on the concrete task of that particular day.

168
00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:50.200
Frankl's most often repeated observation comes out of what he saw in the camp: he writes that the likelihood of survival was not determined by physical endurance alone,

169
00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:56.730
and that those who had a piece of work waiting to be completed, or a person waiting for them, held out far longer.

170
00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:09.660
Meaning, for him, is not an abstract formula but something particular to each person, and it comes from three places: a work done or a thing created, a person loved or an experience lived,

171
00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:14.270
and the attitude taken in the face of a suffering that cannot be changed.

172
00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:19.040
All three require not the search for something, but stepping in underneath something.

173
00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:24.610
One of the most persistent followers of this idea in our own history of thought is Nurettin Topçu.

174
00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:34.340
Topçu, who completed his doctorate in philosophy at the Sorbonne in nineteen thirty-four, wrote a thesis that was translated into Turkish under the title The Ethics of Rebellion.

175
00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:44.390
What Topçu calls rebellion is not destructiveness but the moment a person stops conforming to the crowd and living by ready-made moulds, and takes responsibility for his own act.

176
00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:53.000
For him morality is not something thought but something done; a person becomes a genuine self only to the degree that he takes responsibility.

177
00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:01.930
This looks toward the same place as Frankl's idea of answering the question life puts to us: meaning is the shape of the burden a person takes upon himself.

178
00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:07.020
Now let us return to Tolstoy, because we have not told the end of his story.

179
00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:18.350
After those years in which he hid the cord he did not kill himself, and he died in nineteen ten at the age of eighty-two, at a railway station; that is, he lived more than thirty years beyond that dark period.

180
00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:23.600
According to what he says in A Confession, what pulled him out was not a new philosophy.

181
00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:30.290
He looked at the educated and well-off people around him and saw that they too, like him, had nothing to hold on to.

182
00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:34.260
Then he looked at the ordinary peasants working the same soil.

183
00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:39.830
Their lives were harder, poorer, shorter; but they did not treat living as a problem.

184
00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:47.160
Tolstoy read this not as superiority of intellect but as a bond: they were not thinking about life, they were carrying it.

185
00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:52.090
The burden itself was concrete enough to drown out the question of why the burden existed.

186
00:18:52.090 --> 00:19:01.820
This does not mean "stop thinking and get to work." But it does say this: meaning is not an answer standing above life and waiting to be discovered; it is something inside life,

187
00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:05.310
shaped by the responsibilities a person takes on.

188
00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:12.320
Al-Ghazali left his chair and came back to the same chair eleven years later with a different intention.

189
00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:16.770
Camus chose to keep pushing the rock in a world that offered him no guarantee.

190
00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:22.980
Tolstoy silenced his question not by solving it but by binding himself to something larger than himself.

191
00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:27.270
The place all three arrived at was not a laboratory but an ordinary day.

192
00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:34.440
And here is where the most annoying feature of the feeling of emptiness comes out: its remedy is usually not magnificent.

193
00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:39.530
What makes a person's life meaningful is most often too small to be worth recounting.

194
00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:43.820
Getting up in the morning and doing your work genuinely well.

195
00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:47.950
Being present beside someone you are responsible for.

196
00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:51.600
Calling the parent you have not called in months.

197
00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:57.650
Standing in front of a student, an apprentice, a child, and passing on something you know.

198
00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:00.660
Taking on a task in your own neighbourhood.

199
00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:07.350
None of this looks like "the meaning of life," because we assume the meaning of life will be something large, one-off and luminous.

200
00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:19.960
Yet the shared discovery of the man who hid the cord, the man who pushed the rock and the man who left the chair is this: meaning does not sit in a single place, it sits scattered through the work being done.

201
00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:26.330
So the answer to "everything is fine, why does it feel empty" is not to find the thing that is missing.

202
00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.500
Perhaps the question is put together wrongly.

203
00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.830
The things that are fine are the things we possess.

204
00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:39.040
The empty place is the place of what we give, what we take on, what we name and then step underneath.

205
00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:43.490
However long the first list grows, it does not fill the second.

206
00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:48.740
And the second is filled not by searching, but by getting under something tomorrow morning.

207
00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:52.390
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

208
00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.720
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
